    "updated_at": "2021-07-28T13:41:40Z",
    "body": "> @j9t see https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-http-and-lang for various reasons to use the attribute and avoid relying on the HTTP header, in the rare case that one is even available.\r\n\r\nI’ve provided the [German translation](https://meiert.com/de/w3/International/questions/qa-http-and-lang/) to that very document, and what makes this even weirder is that we’ve specifically [talked about this issue](https://meiert.com/en/blog/html-and-language/#comment-239196), @r12a. This is a super-weird response, and as is, I find it patronizing.\r\n\r\nOn the matter of language declaration and detection, I long hold a clear view on this matter. Instead of getting millions of editors and developers to declare languages and changes of language, I strongly believe in _making language declaration optional and getting software to do the detection job_. Language detection has become increasingly better and we’ve probably reached a point where pushing on manual language tagging is preventing progress on the UA/AT side. This is not the right spot to discuss this, personally I don’t have to discuss this right now, either (I’ve been raising it every few years), but I’m happy to continue conversations about this at any time.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
