    "created_at": "2021-07-29T03:47:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-07-29T03:47:21Z",
    "body": "> On the matter of language declaration and detection, I long hold a clear view on this matter. Instead of getting millions of editors and developers to declare languages and changes of language, I strongly believe in _making language declaration optional and getting software to do the detection job_.\r\n\r\n@j9t: I think getting software to do the detection job is a valid goal for many use cases, but strong believes don't actually create or change software. It's difficult to second-guess why browsers and other software don't yet use language detection, but they must have their reasons.\r\n\r\nThese are just wild guesses, but I could imagine some of the following reasons  (or a combination of these, and some others):\r\n- UA makers have other stuff on their plate with higher priorities.\r\n- Not all UA makers have the same ease of access to the necessary data.\r\n- Not all UA makers have the necessary expertise to implement and test what are essentially heuristics.\r\n- There is a concern that the positive effect of right guesses would be destroyed by the negative effect of wrong guesses (users may be, or may be perceived to be, more sensitive to wrong guesses than to right guesses).\r\n- Language detection works well for a given set of languages, but there will always be languages not in that set, which will suffer.\r\n- If different browsers handle different sets of languages, that would create unnecessary variation.\r\n- Language detection works well for longer texts, but not for short pieces or single words. Those would have to be tagged anyway.\r\n- There is no mechanism to access guessed languages in CSS. There may be a need for some new syntax in CSS at some point.\r\n- There is the question of priority: Both tagged and guessed language can be wrong.\r\n\r\nI agree with @bathos and @annevk that no change in the text is needed.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
